#!/bin/bash
#
# TC-008: refuse a staged test assertion that compares against a bare literal a construction site
# has already made unique.
#
# The failure this exists to catch is not hypothetical in this repository. Commit `e10f7cb` fixed
# four fixtures and three of them were the same shape: a value uniquified where it was built, and
# the assertion left comparing against the old bare literal.
#
#   -  XCTAssertEqual(try res.content.decode(AdvertiserMeResponse.self).email, "adv3@e.com")
#
# The address had grown a UUID suffix at the construction site. The assertion kept the literal, so
# it tested that a generated value equalled a string nothing produced any more. Two more like it in
# `CtrlWaitlistTests` and `VenueScanEndpointTests`, one of which compared two independent `UUID()`
# values that "could never match".
#
# WHAT IT LOOKS FOR, and it is deliberately narrow. A staged line that is an XCTAssertEqual against
# a quoted literal, where the same file also builds that kind of value with a uniquifier. A blunter
# rule would flag every assertion in the suite and be turned off within a day, which is the usual
# fate of a checker that cries wolf.
#
# HOW TO OVERRIDE, because a legitimate case exists: a literal that IS the specification, a frozen
# wire format or a fixed error string. Put `// literal-ok:` and a reason on the line above.
set -euo pipefail

STAGED="$(git diff --cached --name-only --diff-filter=ACM | grep -E '(Tests?|Spec)\.swift$' || true)"
[ -z "$STAGED" ] && exit 0

FOUND=0
for FILE in $STAGED; do
  [ -f "$FILE" ] || continue
  # Only files that actually uniquify something. Elsewhere a literal is just a literal.
  grep -qE 'UUID\(\)|Date\(\)\.timeIntervalSince|\\\(uniqu|randomElement|\.random\(' "$FILE" || continue
  while IFS=: read -r LINE TEXT; do
    [ -z "$LINE" ] && continue
    PREVIOUS="$(sed -n "$((LINE - 1))p" "$FILE" 2>/dev/null || true)"
    case "$PREVIOUS" in *literal-ok:*) continue ;; esac
    echo "  $FILE:$LINE"
    echo "      ${TEXT#"${TEXT%%[![:space:]]*}"}"
    FOUND=1
  # `.*` and not `[^)]*`, and this cost a round trip to learn.
  #
  # The first version used `XCTAssertEqual\([^)]*"` so as to stay inside the call's parentheses.
  # That pattern cannot cross a closing paren, so it missed
  #
  #   XCTAssertEqual(try res.content.decode(AdvertiserMeResponse.self).email, "adv3@e.com")
  #
  # because of the `)` in `decode(...)`. A nested call is the ordinary shape of the very defect
  # this hook was written for, so the checker passed the one line it exists to catch. Caught by
  # running it against that line rather than by reading it.
  done < <(grep -nE 'XCTAssertEqual\(.*"[^"]+"' "$FILE" || true)
done

if [ "$FOUND" = "1" ]; then
  cat >&2 <<'MESSAGE'

This commit stages a test that compares against a bare literal, in a file that also generates
unique values. That is the exact shape of the three fixture defects e10f7cb had to fix: the
construction site grew a uniquifier and the assertion kept the old string, so the test compared a
generated value against something nothing produces any more.

Bind the generated value once and assert against the binding.

If the literal IS the specification, a frozen wire format or a fixed error string, say so on the
line above and this will let it through:

    // literal-ok: the wire format fixes this string

MESSAGE
  exit 1
fi
exit 0
